from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["mary"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Mary was", """Mary was the mother of Jesus. She was a young woman from Nazareth, a small village in Galilee, and she was engaged to a carpenter named Joseph, a descendant of King David. The Bible does not give her age. Most scholars think she was a teenager, as was usual for engagements at the time. Luke says she was a relative of Elizabeth, who was married to a priest named Zechariah.

Nothing in the Bible says she was rich or famous. She was an ordinary girl in an out-of-the-way village. That is the point of her story: God chose someone no one would have noticed.""", ("Luke", 1)),
("Why her story matters", """Mary’s “yes” to God, in the face of danger and gossip, is one of the great acts of faith in the Bible. She is also the only person who is present at both the birth and the death of Jesus.""")],
cards=[
C("The angel Gabriel", "Gabriel told Mary that she would bear the Son of God. She asked how, and then said, “Let it be to me according to your word.”",
"""In the sixth month of Elizabeth’s pregnancy, God sent the angel Gabriel to Nazareth. He said, “Greetings, O favored one, the Lord is with you!” Mary “was greatly troubled at the saying, and tried to discern what sort of greeting this might be.” Gabriel said, “Do not be afraid, Mary, for you have found favor with God. And behold, you will conceive in your womb and bear a son, and you shall call his name Jesus. He will be great and will be called the Son of the Most High. And the Lord God will give to him the throne of his father David, and he will reign over the house of Jacob forever, and of his kingdom there will be no end.”

Mary asked a plain question: “How will this be, since I am a virgin?” Gabriel answered, “The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you; therefore the child to be born will be called holy, the Son of God.” He added that Elizabeth, who was old, was also pregnant: “For nothing will be impossible with God.” Mary said, “Behold, I am the servant of the Lord; let it be to me according to your word.” She knew it could cost her everything. An unmarried pregnant woman could be shamed or even stoned. She said yes anyway.""", ("Luke", 1)),
C("Joseph’s dream and Elizabeth’s house", "Joseph planned to quietly end the engagement, but an angel told him to take Mary as his wife. Mary visited Elizabeth and sang a song of praise.",
"""Mary went quickly to the hill country of Judea to visit Elizabeth. When she greeted her, the baby in Elizabeth’s womb, the future John the Baptist, leaped. Elizabeth, filled with the Holy Spirit, cried out, “Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the fruit of your womb! And why is this granted me that the mother of my Lord should come to me?... Blessed is she who believed that there would be a fulfillment of what was spoken to her from the Lord.”

Mary answered with a song, called the Magnificat: “My soul magnifies the Lord, and my spirit rejoices in God my Savior, for he has looked on the humble estate of his servant... He has brought down the mighty from their thrones and exalted those of humble estate; he has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich he has sent away empty.” She stayed about three months and then went home.

Back in Nazareth, Joseph learned that she was pregnant. He was a righteous man and did not want to shame her publicly, so he planned to divorce her quietly. Then an angel appeared to him in a dream: “Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary as your wife, for that which is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit.” He woke up and did as the angel said.""", ("Matthew", 1)),
C("Bethlehem and the shepherds", "Mary gave birth to Jesus in Bethlehem and laid him in a manger. Shepherds came, and Mary treasured all these things.",
"""The Roman emperor Augustus ordered a census, so Joseph and Mary, who was very pregnant, traveled about ninety miles from Nazareth to Bethlehem, Joseph’s ancestral town. “And while they were there, the time came for her to give birth. And she gave birth to her firstborn son and wrapped him in swaddling cloths and laid him in a manger, because there was no place for them in the inn.”

That night, an angel appeared to shepherds in the fields: “Fear not, for behold, I bring you good news of great joy that will be for all the people. For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Savior, who is Christ the Lord.” The shepherds hurried to Bethlehem and found Mary, Joseph and the baby lying in a manger. They told everyone what the angels had said. “But Mary treasured up all these things, pondering them in her heart.” She would keep doing that for the rest of her life.""", ("Luke", 2)),
C("Simeon’s warning, the wise men and Egypt", "Simeon told Mary a sword would pierce her soul. Warned of Herod, the family fled to Egypt.",
"""Forty days after the birth, Mary and Joseph brought Jesus to the Temple in Jerusalem to be presented to the Lord. They offered two young pigeons, the offering of the poor. An old man named Simeon, who had been promised that he would see the Messiah before he died, took the baby in his arms and praised God. Then he said to Mary, “Behold, this child is appointed for the fall and rising of many in Israel, and for a sign that is opposed (and a sword will pierce through your own soul also), so that thoughts from many hearts may be revealed.” An elderly prophetess named Anna also gave thanks.

Later, wise men from the East came to Jerusalem looking for the king of the Jews. They found the child in a house with Mary his mother, bowed down and gave Him gold, frankincense and myrrh. King Herod wanted to kill the child. An angel warned Joseph in a dream, and they fled to Egypt that night. They stayed until Herod died, then settled in Nazareth.""", ("Luke", 2)),
C("The boy in the Temple and the wedding at Cana", "Mary and Joseph found twelve-year-old Jesus in the Temple. Years later, Mary told the servants at Cana, “Do whatever he tells you.”",
"""When Jesus was twelve, the family went to Jerusalem for Passover. On the way home, they discovered He was not with the group. They searched for three days and found Him in the Temple, sitting among the teachers, listening and asking questions. Mary said, “Son, why have you treated us so? Behold, your father and I have been searching for you in great distress.” Jesus said, “Why were you looking for me? Did you not know that I must be in my Father’s house?” They did not understand. He went home with them and “was submissive to them. And his mother treasured up all these things in her heart.”

At the beginning of His public ministry, Mary was at a wedding at Cana. The wine ran out, which was a serious embarrassment for the hosts. Mary told Jesus, “They have no wine.” He answered, “Woman, what does this have to do with me? My hour has not yet come.” Mary turned to the servants: “Do whatever he tells you.” Jesus had them fill six stone jars with water, and it became wine. These are the last recorded words of Mary in the Gospels. They are the best advice she could give.""", ("John", 2)),
C("At the cross and in the upper room", "Mary stood at the cross while Jesus died. Jesus gave her into John’s care. She was with the believers in the upper room.",
"""Mary is mentioned a few times during Jesus’ ministry, and once when she and His brothers came to find Him and He said that those who do the will of God are His family. Then she appears at the cross. “Standing by the cross of Jesus were his mother and his mother’s sister, Mary the wife of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene.” The sword that Simeon had spoken about had arrived.

Even in His last hours, Jesus took care of her. “When Jesus saw his mother and the disciple whom he loved standing nearby, he said to his mother, ‘Woman, behold, your son!’ Then he said to the disciple, ‘Behold, your mother!’ And from that hour the disciple took her to his own home.”

After the resurrection and ascension, Acts 1 says the apostles were “devoting themselves to prayer, together with the women and Mary the mother of Jesus, and his brothers.” It is the last time the Bible mentions her. The Bible does not say how or when she died. She began as a young girl who said yes, and she ended up among the first believers praying in Jerusalem.""", ("Acts", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Mary shows us what faith looks like. She did not understand everything, and she asked honest questions. But when God’s will became clear, she said, “Let it be to me according to your word.” She trusted God with her reputation, her plans and her future.

She also shows us what faithful presence looks like. She was at the manger, at Cana, at the cross and in the upper room. She kept watching, listening and treasuring. Following Jesus is not only a moment of decision but a lifetime of staying close to Him.

And her last words in the Gospels are a command we can all take to heart: “Do whatever he tells you.” That is still the simplest summary of what it means to be a disciple.""", ("John", 2))])

LONG["moses"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Moses was", """Moses is the greatest prophet of the Old Testament and the man God used to bring Israel out of slavery in Egypt. He lived about 1400 to 1250 BC, though scholars differ on the dates. He was born a Hebrew, raised as an Egyptian prince and lived forty years as a shepherd in the desert before God called him. The first five books of the Bible, the Torah, are traditionally ascribed to him.

The Bible says, “There has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom the Lord knew face to face.”""", ("Deuteronomy", 34)),
("Why his story matters", """Moses’ story is the story of the Exodus, the great rescue that Israel remembered for a thousand years and that the New Testament uses to explain what Jesus did. It also includes the Ten Commandments, the Passover and the crossing of the Red Sea.""")],
cards=[
C("Saved from the river", "Pharaoh ordered Hebrew baby boys to be killed. Moses’ mother hid him in a basket, and Pharaoh’s daughter found him.",
"""The Israelites had been in Egypt for generations and had grown into a large people. A new Pharaoh feared them, made them slaves and finally ordered that every Hebrew baby boy be thrown into the Nile. A woman from the tribe of Levi had a son and hid him for three months. When she could hide him no longer, she made a basket of papyrus, coated it with tar, put the baby inside and set it among the reeds at the river’s edge. His sister Miriam watched from a distance.

Pharaoh’s daughter came to bathe, saw the basket and opened it. “Behold, the baby was crying. She took pity on him and said, ‘This is one of the Hebrews’ children.’” Miriam stepped out and offered to find a nurse, and brought the baby’s own mother. The princess paid her to nurse him. When he was older, he was brought to the palace and became her son. She named him Moses, “because I drew him out of the water.” He grew up with the best education in Egypt, but he knew he was a Hebrew.""", ("Exodus", 2)),
C("Flight to the desert", "Moses killed an Egyptian who was beating a Hebrew and had to flee. He became a shepherd in Midian for forty years.",
"""When Moses was grown, he went out to see his people and saw an Egyptian beating a Hebrew slave. He looked around, saw no one, and killed the Egyptian, hiding the body in the sand. The next day, he tried to stop two Hebrews from fighting, and one said, “Who made you a prince and a judge over us? Do you mean to kill me as you killed the Egyptian?” Moses was afraid, and when Pharaoh heard, he tried to kill Moses. Moses fled.

He went to Midian, east of Egypt, and sat by a well. He helped seven sisters, the daughters of the priest Jethro, water their flock against some shepherds who drove them away. Jethro gave him his daughter Zipporah as his wife, and they had a son, Gershom, whose name means “a sojourner there.” Moses spent forty years in the desert as a shepherd. The prince of Egypt had become a nobody tending sheep. It was the training he needed: he learned the wilderness he would one day lead a nation through.""", ("Exodus", 2)),
C("The burning bush", "God spoke to Moses from a bush that burned but was not consumed and sent him to free Israel. Moses made excuses.",
"""At the age of eighty, Moses was tending Jethro’s flock near Mount Horeb, also called Sinai, when he saw a bush on fire that was not burning up. He went to look. God called from the bush, “Moses, Moses!” “Here I am.” “Do not come near; take your sandals off your feet, for the place on which you are standing is holy ground. I am the God of your father, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.” Moses hid his face.

God said, “I have surely seen the affliction of my people who are in Egypt... So now, go, I will send you to Pharaoh that you may bring my people, the children of Israel, out of Egypt.” Moses made excuse after excuse. “Who am I?” “I will be with you.” “What is your name?” “I AM WHO I AM.” “What if they do not believe me?” God gave him signs: the staff that became a snake and a hand that turned leprous. “I am not eloquent.” “Who has made man’s mouth?... Now therefore go, and I will be with your mouth.” At last Moses said, “Send someone else.” God was angry, but He gave him his brother Aaron as a spokesman.""", ("Exodus", 3)),
C("The plagues and the Passover", "Pharaoh refused to let Israel go, and God sent ten plagues on Egypt. The last was the death of the firstborn, and Israel was spared by the blood of a lamb.",
"""Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh: “Let my people go, that they may hold a feast to me in the wilderness.” Pharaoh said, “Who is the Lord, that I should obey his voice?... I do not know the Lord, and moreover, I will not let Israel go.” He made the slaves’ work even harder. Then came the plagues: the Nile turned to blood, frogs, gnats, flies, the death of livestock, boils, hail, locusts and three days of darkness. Each time, Pharaoh promised and then changed his mind.

The tenth plague was the worst. God told Israel that each family should take an unblemished lamb, kill it at twilight, put its blood on the doorposts and eat the meal in haste, ready to travel. “The blood shall be a sign for you, on the houses where you are. And when I see the blood, I will pass over you, and no plague will befall you to destroy you.” At midnight the firstborn of Egypt died, from Pharaoh’s son to the prisoner’s. Pharaoh called Moses and said, “Up, go out from among my people.” Israel has celebrated the Passover ever since. The New Testament calls Jesus “our Passover lamb.”""", ("Exodus", 12)),
C("The Red Sea and Sinai", "God parted the sea so Israel could cross on dry ground. At Mount Sinai, God gave the Ten Commandments.",
"""About 600,000 men, along with women and children, left Egypt, led by a pillar of cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night. Then Pharaoh changed his mind and chased them with chariots. Israel was trapped between the army and the Red Sea. The people cried out in fear. Moses said, “Fear not, stand firm, and see the salvation of the Lord.” God told him to lift his staff over the sea. “The Lord drove the sea back by a strong east wind all night and made the sea dry land, and the waters were divided.” Israel crossed on dry ground. When the Egyptians followed, the waters returned. Moses and Miriam led the people in a song.

Three months later, they reached Mount Sinai. God spoke from the mountain in thunder, lightning, smoke and a loud trumpet. He gave the Ten Commandments, beginning, “I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of slavery.” The commandments came after the rescue. Moses was on the mountain forty days, and God gave him the instructions for the tabernacle and the law.""", ("Exodus", 20)),
C("The golden calf and the wilderness", "While Moses was on the mountain, the people made a golden calf. Moses pleaded for them. Israel wandered forty years.",
"""While Moses was on the mountain, the people grew restless and asked Aaron, “Make us gods who shall go before us.” Aaron collected their gold and made a calf. Moses came down with the two tablets, saw the dancing and smashed them. God was ready to destroy the people. Moses prayed, “Alas, this people has sinned a great sin... But now, if you will forgive their sin—but if not, please blot me out of your book that you have written.” God relented. Moses asked, “Please show me your glory.” God said no one can see His face and live, but He let His goodness pass by and said, “The Lord, the Lord, a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger, and abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness.”

The people later refused to enter the Promised Land because of the fearful report of ten of the twelve spies. As a result, they wandered forty years in the wilderness until that generation had passed. Moses was patient with them but not perfect. At Meribah, he struck a rock in anger instead of speaking to it as God had commanded, and God told him he would not enter the land.""", ("Exodus", 32)),
C("Mount Nebo", "Moses gave final speeches to Israel, named Joshua as leader and saw the Promised Land from a mountain before he died at 120.",
"""At the end of the forty years, Israel camped east of the Jordan. Moses, now 120, gave a long farewell in the book of Deuteronomy. He told them to remember what God had done, to love the Lord with all their heart, soul and might, and to teach these words to their children. “I have set before you life and death, blessing and curse. Therefore choose life, that you and your offspring may live.” He gave a blessing to each tribe and appointed Joshua, “Be strong and courageous.”

God told him to climb Mount Nebo. From the top, he could see the whole land that had been promised to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. “I have let you see it with your eyes, but you shall not go over there.” “So Moses the servant of the Lord died there in the land of Moab, according to the word of the Lord... His eye was undimmed, and his vigor unabated.” God buried him, and no one knows where. Centuries later, Moses appeared with Elijah on a mountain beside Jesus at the Transfiguration, finally in the land.""", ("Deuteronomy", 34))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Moses’ life has three parts: forty years as a prince, forty years as a shepherd and forty years as a leader. God did not waste any of them. The hard years in the desert prepared him for what came next. If you feel you are in a waiting season, it may be preparation.

Moses also shows that God uses people who feel unqualified. He said, “Who am I?” and “I am not eloquent,” and God said, “I will be with you.” What matters is not our strength but His presence.

Finally, the whole Exodus story points to Jesus. A lamb’s blood saves from death. A leader brings the people out of slavery. The law given at a mountain is fulfilled in Him. The Bible says, “The law was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ.”""", ("John", 1))])
